  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|3n|<10}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|3q|<10}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|3x|<10}$",
  "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x|<10 \\\\ 3|x|<10 \\\\ |x|<\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{10}{3}<x<\\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\frac{10}{3},\\frac{10}{3}).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|3n|<10$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|3q|<10$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B\u2282C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"",
